--- Rosetta Code: Hello world/Newbie
--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Hello_world/Newbie
--- A guide for a programmer new to Crow: install it, write the Hello world/Text
--- program in any text editor, run it both ways, and find its output in the terminal.
---
--- 1. Install. On a Mac (Apple silicon) or a Linux machine (x86_64), and on Windows
---    inside WSL, one line fetches the prebuilt binary into ~/.crow/bin and checks
---    it against its published hash:
---
---        curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/agncrow/crow/main/install.sh | bash
---
---    It ends by printing the line that puts ~/.crow/bin on your PATH: add that line
---    to your shell profile, open a new terminal, and check with
---
---        crow -v
---
---    which prints the version. On a machine the release has no binary for, build
---    Crow from its source (the script installs a C toolchain and Rust if missing):
---
---        git clone https://github.com/agncrow/crow.git
---        cd crow
---        ./install.sh --source
---
--- 2. Write the program. A Crow program is a plain text file ending in .co, so any
---    text editor will do: save this file as hello.co. VS Code, Cursor and VSCodium
---    have the extension "Crow" by crow-lang, which runs Crow's own language server
---    (crow lsp) to show errors as you type; Neovim, Helix and Zed reach the same
---    server (docs/crow-lsp.md in the repository has the setup).
---
--- 3. Run it, from the directory that holds hello.co. There are two ways, and they
---    print the same:
---
---        crow run -i hello.co      interpreted: starts at once
---        crow run hello.co         compiled: translated to Rust, built, run natively
---
---    Compiling needs a Rust toolchain: `crow setup` fetches a pinned one into
---    ~/.crow once (about 175 MB), and so does the first compiled run. Until it is
---    there, `crow run` says so and interprets instead.
---
--- 4. Read the output. It is printed in the same terminal, on a line of its own:
---
---        Hello world!
---
--- Further reading: the Crow Book, https://agncrow.github.io/crow/, starts with these
--- steps and goes on through the language; the repository's README.md has the same
--- install line, and `crow help` lists every command.
---
--- The program itself is one line. log[...] prints a value on its own line: a call
--- takes square brackets, and text is written in single quotes.

log['Hello world!']
